static const char* const kAboutHtml = "<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\"><b>Physics Lab</b></div><div class=\"lrnSm\">A playground for seeing how the universe works: real orbits and atoms. It is built on real physics, and where something is simplified, it says so.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The developer:</b> Physics Lab is made by <b>Alex</b>, one idea and one update at a time. If you find a bug or have an idea, tell Alex.</p><p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Built with Claude:</b> Alex decides what goes in and comes up with the ideas. It is built together with Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, which helps write the code, check the facts and test everything.</p><div class=\"lrnP srcBox\"><b>Where the facts come from:</b> planet positions come from NASA/JPL orbital data, and the isotope table is based on the NUBASE nuclear data (through the mendeleev package). Facts in the guides are checked against several sources, and where the sources disagree the text says so. The main ones:<div class=\"srcH\">🪐 Space and orbits</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/approx_pos.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: planet positions (Keplerian elements, 1800–2050)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: planetary fact sheet</a> (sizes, masses and temperatures; the planets&rsquo; gravity is from JPL, below)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: Small-Body Database</a> (dwarf planets, asteroids, comets)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/phys_par.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: planetary physical parameters</a> (each planet&rsquo;s surface gravity at the equator, read from the page on 2026-10-03; the cards give it in g, with Earth&rsquo;s 9.80 m/s&sup2; on the same page as 1 g)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons, the Sun&rsquo;s header (Horizons API, COMMAND 10, read 2026-10-03): &ldquo;Solar radius (IAU2015) = 695700 km&rdquo;, the nominal solar radius the lab uses; and &ldquo;Adopted sid. rot. per. = 25.38 d&rdquo;, the Sun&rsquo;s standard turning period on its card</li><li>NASA/JPL Small-Body Database records of the six famous comets (SBDB API, read 2026-10-03: orbit 75 Halley, 226 Hale&ndash;Bopp, 31 NEOWISE, K273/20 Encke, 32 Swift&ndash;Tuttle; 67P: orbit K284/1 as Horizons gives it at 2021-11-02), and Horizons position vectors of the comets at two dates each, used to check them; the comets&rsquo; nucleus sizes are the Small-Body Database&rsquo;s diameters (it has none for NEOWISE). Each comet is one fixed two-body orbit, so it drifts from the real one away from the record&rsquo;s date</li><li>NASA/JPL astrodynamic parameters page, the astronomical unit: 149,597,870,700 m exactly, which the lab uses for every AU, and the speed of light, 299,792,458 m/s (quoted there as a 2018 CODATA value). The page names the IAU 2012 resolution as B1, the owner's decision calls it B2; the IAU text itself (and the SYRTE page about it) could not be opened, so it was not read. The lab used the web version's 149.6e9 m before 0.10.5 stage 4. A light year is worked out as that speed times a 365.25-day year, in AU (the IAU value was not looked up)</li><li>Figures with no named source, marked rough in the app or stated as the app's own rule: the Sun's surface temperature, habitable zone, frost line and lifetime; the Sun's 25-day equator and 34-day pole turning; the counts and masses on the belt cards (asteroid, Trojan, Kuiper, Oort); NEOWISE's 5 km nucleus; the 30 % reflected-light assumption for temperatures without an atmosphere; the Moon's phase names between the four main phases (this app's own cut-offs)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/phys_par/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: planetary satellite physical parameters</a> and <a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/elem/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">mean elements</a> (the moons: sizes and orbits)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/astro_par.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: astrodynamic parameters</a> (GM of the Sun and of each planet with its moons, DE440 values, for &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo;; first read from an excerpt of the page, then read from the page itself and matched the code, 2026-10-03)</li><li>Park et al., 2021, The Astronomical Journal: the DE440 and DE441 planetary and lunar ephemerides (the paper behind those DE440 values)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd-api.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/horizons.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: Horizons API documentation</a> (how the Horizons vector tables used for the start of &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo; are asked for)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/de440_de441.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: DE440 and DE441</a> (the note on the two ephemerides: Horizons&rsquo; default is DE441, which spans &minus;13200 to +17191 and is a little less accurate than DE440 for the present century, mainly for the Moon; the page was not opened in the session that wrote this, the figures are as the owner read them; the Real note&rsquo;s errors are against DE441, not DE440)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons data retrieved 2026-10-03 (Horizons API, ephemeris DE441: position and velocity of the Sun and of each planet system, 1 January 12:00 TDB of every year from 1800 to 2100; the start of &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo; is its 1 January 2026 row, the other rows check the motion)</li><li><a href=\"https://cdsarc.cds.unistra.fr/ftp/VI/79/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Chapront-Touz&eacute; and Chapront, 1988, Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics 190, 342: the lunar theory ELP2000-82B</a> (CDS catalogue VI/79: the series and the Fortran routine it is written from; the Moon&rsquo;s position, from the 487 largest of its 37,872 terms. It is a 1988 theory fitted to JPL&rsquo;s older DE200, so it is less accurate than JPL&rsquo;s own ephemerides: against Horizons (DE441) at the dates checked between 1800 and 2100 it is within 5.2 km (2.9 arcseconds) at worst. Real planet physics in the Orbit Lab draws Earth&rsquo;s Moon from it: its direction and phase are real, its distance is drawn exaggerated like every moon&rsquo;s; positions are geometric (no light time), dates are used as TDB, and outside 1800 to 1 Jan 2100 it is not used. The phase names: the four main phases are exact, the names in between (crescent, gibbous) use this app&rsquo;s own cut-offs, at 2, 40, 60 and 98 % lit)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons data retrieved 2026-10-03, the Moon table (Horizons API, ephemeris DE441: the position of the Moon and the Sun seen from the Earth&rsquo;s centre, every 5 years from 1800 to 2100, daily for 60 days from 2025-12-02 and hourly on 2026-01-02 to 04; used only to check the ELP2000-82B Moon and its phases)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons data retrieved 2026-10-03, the Earth table (Horizons API, ephemeris DE441: the position of the Earth&rsquo;s centre seen from the Sun&rsquo;s centre, 1 January 12:00 TDB of every year from 1800 to 2100; used only to check where Real planet physics puts Earth)</li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1086/115978\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wisdom &amp; Holman, 1991: Symplectic maps for the N-body problem</a> (the method that moves the planets in &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo;)</li><li>Duncan, Levison &amp; Lee, 1998: A multiple time step symplectic algorithm for integrating close encounters (The Astronomical Journal; the variables that method is written in)</li><li>Bate, Mueller &amp; White, 1971: <i>Fundamentals of Astrodynamics</i> (moving a planet along its Kepler orbit with universal variables)</li><li>Clemence, 1947: The relativity effect in planetary motions (Reviews of Modern Physics; the 532 arcseconds per century of Mercury&rsquo;s perihelion advance from the other planets, used to check &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo;)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtamayo/reboundx/blob/main/src/gr_potential.c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">REBOUNDx, the file gr_potential.c</a> (Tamayo, Rein, Shi &amp; Hernandez, 2020, Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society 491, 2885: the simple potential of the Sun behind &ldquo;General relativity (1PN)&rdquo; for the planets; the formula was read in that source file)</li><li>Nobili &amp; Roxburgh, 1986: Simulation of general relativistic corrections in long term numerical integrations of planetary orbits (IAU Symposium 114; the idea of that potential; the paper itself was not read)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.7377\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Will, 2014: The confrontation between general relativity and experiment</a> (Living Reviews in Relativity 17; Mercury&rsquo;s relativistic perihelion advance of 42.98 arcseconds per century, used to check &ldquo;General relativity (1PN)&rdquo;; read from a search excerpt, not from the paper)</li><li><a href=\"https://dlmf.nist.gov/14.30\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, section 14.30: spherical harmonics</a> (the definition and the Condon&ndash;Shortley phase of Y<sub>lm</sub> behind the complex orbitals m = +1, &minus;1, &hellip; in the Atom Lab; the page itself was not opened from the session that wrote this)</li><li>SciPy 1.17.1 (<code>scipy.special.sph_harm_y</code>) and scikit-image 0.26.0 (<code>skimage.color.lab2rgb</code>): used only in the tests, as independent checks of those spherical harmonics and of the phase colour wheel</li><li>CIE 1976 L*a*b* colour space and the sRGB standard (IEC 61966-2-1): the phase colour wheel is a circle of constant lightness in L*a*b*, so the colour shows the phase and not a brightness pattern (the formulas are the standard ones, written from knowledge, not looked up here)</li><li>Richard Fitzpatrick, <i>Introductory Quantum Mechanics</i> (lecture notes, University of Texas at Austin), the section on the 2P&ndash;1S transitions in hydrogen: the matrix element &lang;1s|z|2p&rang; = 128&radic;2/243 a<sub>0</sub> that sets the size of the oscillating dipole in the Atom Lab&rsquo;s quantum beats (the page was not opened from the session that wrote this; the number was checked by numerical integration in the tests)</li><li>CODATA 2022 recommended values (NIST): the Planck constant, the Rydberg energy and the proton&ndash;electron mass ratio, used in the tests to check the beat period h/(E<sub>2</sub>&minus;E<sub>1</sub>) (the values were read from the NIST pages on 2026-10-04, see the next entry)</li><li>David J. Griffiths, <i>Introduction to Quantum Mechanics</i>, the chapter on time-dependent perturbation theory (the two-level system): the textbook picture of a transition as a mixing of two states, which the Atom Lab&rsquo;s smooth photon jump follows. It is a picture, not a calculation of how a photon is absorbed (the book was not opened from the session that wrote this)</li><li>CODATA 2022 recommended values (NIST, the pages <code>physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?</code> followed by bohrrada0, hrev, aut, alph, rydhcev, mec2mev, meu, mpsme, ukg, muc2mev, me, mp, mn, mpu, mnu, k, h, c and e, read on 2026-10-04; each page says &ldquo;2022 CODATA recommended values&rdquo;): the Atom Lab&rsquo;s constants since 0.10.6 stage 2: the Bohr radius 5.29177210544&times;10<sup>&minus;11</sup> m, the Hartree energy 27.211386245981 eV, the atomic unit of time 2.4188843265864&times;10<sup>&minus;17</sup> s (so the atomic unit of speed), the fine-structure constant, the Rydberg energy 13.605693122990 eV, the electron mass energy, the electron, proton and neutron masses (in kg and in u), the atomic mass constant (in kg and in MeV), the proton&ndash;electron mass ratio, the Boltzmann constant, and the exact h, c and e (so h&middot;c = 1239.841984 eV&middot;nm). Before that the lab used the web version&rsquo;s rounded 0.529 &Aring;, 27.211 eV and 24.19 as (the Bohr radius was 0.03 % too small). Not from NIST and not re-read: the mass of the hydrogen-1 atom, 1.00782503223 u (the atomic mass evaluation behind the isotope data), and the proton radius, kept at the web&rsquo;s rounded 0.84 fm (CODATA 2022 gives 0.84075 fm). The tests compare the constants with these values typed in again from the pages, and check that the atomic unit of speed equals the fine-structure constant times c</li><li>Visible light, 380&ndash;780 nm: a convention this app uses (it is the range of the web version&rsquo;s colour code); no source for the range was read. The Atom Lab uses this one range for the spectrum card, the colour of every line and the (UV) and (IR) tags (the spectrum card used 380&ndash;750 nm before 0.10.6 stage 5).</li><li>The Dirac 1s energy of a one-electron atom with a point nucleus, mc&sup2;(1 &minus; &radic;(1 &minus; (Z&alpha;)&sup2;)): used in the heavy-atom readout, and to choose the line (Z = 27, where it is about 1 % above the non-relativistic energy) from which the Atom Lab warns that its non-relativistic model is off. The formula is the standard one, written from knowledge, not looked up here; the percentages are worked out from it by the lab and the tests.</li><li>AME2020, the atomic mass evaluation (Huang, Wang, Kondev, Audi &amp; Naimi, 2021, Chinese Physics C 45, 030002; the table <code>www-nds.iaea.org/amdc/ame2020/mass.mas20.txt</code>, read on 2026-10-04): the binding energies the Atom Lab already used (3,557 nuclides) agree with its atomic masses, and since 0.10.6 stage 6 they give the nucleus&rsquo;s mass for the spectrum lines (its protons and neutrons, from CODATA 2022, minus its binding energy; before that A times the atomic mass unit, which was 0.70 % too light for deuterium and put its D-alpha line 1.3 pm too long). 695 of the nuclides on the neutron slider lie beyond the table and keep the old rough mass. The tests compare the masses with AME2020&rsquo;s atomic masses for H-1, H-2, H-3, He-4, C-12, Fe-56 and U-238.</li><li>NIST Atomic Spectra Database, lines form (<code>physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/ASD/lines1.pl</code>, read on 2026-10-04: the vacuum Ritz wavelengths of the fine-structure components of H I and He II): used only in the tests, to check that the lab&rsquo;s level-averaged, non-relativistic lines (Lyman-alpha, H-alpha, H-beta and two He II lines) lie within those components widened by (Z&alpha;)&sup2;, the size of what the lab leaves out (fine structure, the Lamb shift, spin).</li><li>NIST Atomic Spectra Database (ASD), lines form (<code>physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/ASD/lines1.pl</code>, retrieved on 2026-10-04, vacuum wavelengths, version 5.12 as listed on ASD&rsquo;s version history page): the real measured spectrum beside the lab&rsquo;s, in the Atom Lab&rsquo;s &ldquo;Real spectrum (NIST)&rdquo; card, as two separate comparisons. (a) The hydrogen-like ion of the element (H I, He II, &hellip; Fe XXVI: one electron round a nucleus of charge Z, the ion the lab models): the lines between levels n &le; 6, with the fine-structure components, to check the lab; they are observed wavelengths where ASD has them and otherwise Ritz wavelengths worked out from measured energy levels, each with the uncertainty ASD gives. (b) The neutral atom of the element (H I, He I, &hellip; Fe I): the 30 strongest observed lines in 100&ndash;2500 nm that ASD gives an intensity for (ASD says its relative intensities are qualitative, have no common scale and mean something only within one spectrum, so the lab ranks and draws them within one spectrum only), to show the limit of the lab, which has one electron where a real atom has Z. Spectra ASD does not have (the ions of Z = 111&ndash;118 and the neutral atoms of Fm and heavier) are said to be missing on screen. Since 0.10.8 stage 3 the same ASD lines also give the lab&rsquo;s lifetimes and jump rates: each line&rsquo;s transition probability A (the Einstein A coefficient, the spontaneous-emission rate, in s<sup>&minus;1</sup>), the accuracy code ASD gives it (AAA &le; 0.3 %, AA &le; 1 %, A+ &le; 2 %, A &le; 3 %, B+ &le; 7 %, B &le; 10 %, C+ &le; 18 %, C &le; 25 %, D+ &le; 40 %, D &le; 50 %, E &gt; 50 %; a prime means a multiplet was split assuming pure LS coupling) and its transition type (blank = allowed electric dipole E1; M1, E2, M1+E2, UT = forbidden), all kept from the same retrieval (no new request) and explained by ASD&rsquo;s lines help page, read on 2026-10-04, which also states ASD&rsquo;s own rule for an allowed line (levels of opposite parity, J changing by at most 1). The lab adds only the lifetime 1/&Sigma;A and the branching shares A/&Sigma;A over the allowed jumps to a lower n, and gives no number where ASD has none (no rate is calculated). In that data ASD lists the 1s&ndash;2s line of Cs LV (Z = 55) as allowed although l does not change; the lab follows the selection rule and does not use that value. Cited as NIST asks: Kramida, A., Ralchenko, Yu., Reader, J. and NIST ASD Team (2024). NIST Atomic Spectra Database (version 5.12), [Online]. Available: https://physics.nist.gov/asd. National Institute of Standards and Technology, Gaithersburg, MD. DOI: https://doi.org/10.18434/T4W30F. The Dirac energy formula the tests use to explain the heavy ions&rsquo; difference is the standard one, written from knowledge, not looked up here.</li><li>The Dirac energy levels of a one-electron atom with a point nucleus (the Sommerfeld fine-structure formula, E = mc&sup2;[(1 + (Z&alpha;)&sup2;/(n &minus; &delta;)&sup2;)<sup>&minus;1/2</sup> &minus; 1], &delta; = (j + &frac12;) &minus; &radic;((j + &frac12;)&sup2; &minus; (Z&alpha;)&sup2;)): the Atom Lab&rsquo;s &ldquo;Relativity (Dirac levels)&rdquo; setting (0.10.8, off by default) uses it for the energy levels and lines of the two spectrum cards. The formula is standard textbook material (Hans A. Bethe and Edwin E. Salpeter, <i>Quantum Mechanics of One- and Two-Electron Atoms</i>, listed in this section; Griffiths gives its expansion); it was written from knowledge and not looked up in the session that wrote it, and the tests check it against hand arithmetic, its expansion and the measured NIST lines (the ASD source above: the lab&rsquo;s Dirac lines are matched to ASD&rsquo;s components by n and j, and they land closer than the non-relativistic lines for H I, He II and Fe XXVI). The constants are the CODATA 2022 ones above. The nucleus&rsquo;s motion enters only through the reduced mass in the main energy; the relativistic correction is that of an infinitely heavy nucleus, and no source for the further recoil terms was read. Not in it: the Lamb shift and other QED effects, and the size of the nucleus.</li><li>Nuclear charge radii, the size every nucleus with a measurement is drawn at (0.10.9): the IAEA Nuclear Data Section&rsquo;s table of nuclear charge radii, the file <code>www-nds.iaea.org/radii/charge_radii.csv</code> (retrieved on 2026-10-04, 28,869 bytes). It has 957 rows: 908 isotopes of 90 elements, from hydrogen to curium (Z = 96), with a measured rms charge radius and its uncertainty; 48 more with only a preliminary value, which the lab does not use; and the neutron&rsquo;s row, a negative number (so a mean-square radius in fm&sup2;, not a radius), which the lab leaves out. The file carries no citation text and does not say which edition it is. The compilation behind a table like it is I. Angeli and K. P. Marinova, <i>Atomic Data and Nuclear Data Tables</i> 99 (2013) 69, &ldquo;Table of experimental nuclear ground state charge radii: An update&rdquo; (the title and its 909 isotopes of 92 elements were read from a search listing; the volume and page were not checked on the journal&rsquo;s page, and neither was which edition the IAEA file is). A charge radius is the rms radius of the protons&rsquo; electric charge. Neutrons carry no charge, so it is not the matter radius, and that is why halo nuclei are not drawn from it. The lab draws the nucleus as a uniform ball of radius R = &radic;(5/3) &times; the rms radius (a uniform ball has rms radius &radic;(3/5) R, a calculus result; that the nucleus is a uniform ball is an assumption, real nuclei have soft edges), and the nucleon positions are scaled so that the protons&rsquo; rms radius is the measured one. Where the table has no value the usual rule r = 1.2 A<sup>1/3</sup> fm is kept and the card says so. Hydrogen-1 keeps the CODATA 2022 proton radius above. That helium-4 is smaller than helium-3 and that lithium&rsquo;s radius falls to lithium-9 and rises at lithium-11 are the table&rsquo;s own numbers; the binding energies beside them are the AME2020 ones above. The relation R = &radic;(5/3) &times; rms was written from knowledge and not looked up.</li><li>Nuclear electric quadrupole moments, where the nucleus&rsquo;s shape comes from (0.10.9 stage 2): the IAEA Nuclear Data Section&rsquo;s recommended values, the file <code>www-nds.iaea.org/nuclearmoments/elec_mom_recomm.csv</code> (retrieved on 2026-10-04, 135,013 bytes, 1,155 rows: 599 ground states and 556 isomers; the lab uses the ground states only). Each row names the INDC report indc-nds-0833 and an NSR reference key; the page that states how to cite the table could not be read (a bot challenge), so no further citation is given. The file&rsquo;s second column, headed n.n+n.z, is the mass number (read that way every ground-state row is a known nuclide; read as a neutron count only 53 of 599 are). Values are in barns. Seven rows that are not one clean number (five of them typing errors), 41 values with no sign, four with a tentative sign and 34 with a tentative spin are not used for the shape; a duplicate K-41 row is left out. The table gives the spectroscopic moment, which is zero for spin 0 and 1/2 whatever the shape. The lab turns it into the intrinsic moment with the strong-coupling relation (K = I), Q = Q<sub>0</sub> I(2I &minus; 1) / ((I + 1)(2I + 3)): a model assumption, not a measurement, and a poor one for light nuclei and near closed shells; it is not used where it would need |&beta;<sub>2</sub>| above 0.6 (the lab&rsquo;s own limit, no source). The shape is a uniformly charged body r = R(1 + &beta;<sub>2</sub>Y<sub>20</sub>) whose volume is kept (an assumption), and &beta;<sub>2</sub> is solved from Q<sub>0</sub> and the measured charge radius above. Where there is no usable moment the lab keeps its B(E2) values (its own table of 11 isotopes, whose source the code does not name) or its estimate (Casten&rsquo;s P-factor), and the card says which. The relation and the conversion were written from knowledge, not looked up.</li><li>Nuclear spins, the kind of each half-life and the chart of nuclides (0.11 stage 2): NUBASE2020, F.G. Kondev, M. Wang, W.J. Huang, S. Naimi and G. Audi, Chin. Phys. C45, 030001 (2021), the file <code>www-nds.iaea.org/amdc/ame2020/nubase_4.mas20.txt</code> (retrieved on 2026-10-04, 761,906 bytes; 3,558 ground-state lines: the free neutron and the 3,557 nuclides the chart draws, the same ones the lab already had). The half-lives, decay modes and odds on the chart come from the lab&rsquo;s own table (also NUBASE2020, through the mendeleev package); the file adds the spin and parity of each nuclide, with NUBASE&rsquo;s own marks (directly measured, estimated from systematics, tentative), and tells apart a half-life that is only an extrapolation from systematics (369 nuclides, never shown as a number), a limit, an approximate value and none. The page that states how the table may be cited could not be read (a bot challenge), so the credit is only the citation the file gives. The file&rsquo;s decay column marks some entries with a question mark and does not explain the mark in its header; the app shows such entries as written. The colour of a square is a convention of this lab, not a source: stable where the lab&rsquo;s table says stable (251; NUBASE2020 says stable for 253, and the two extra, krypton-78 and xenon-124, have measured half-lives in the lab&rsquo;s table), otherwise the kind of decay with the largest total odds (a beta-delayed branch counts as its beta); where the odds are not measured, the kind if all listed modes agree, else grey. The cross-check against the file&rsquo;s own decay column agrees for 3,054 of the 3,083 nuclides where it gives usable numbers; in most of the rest the lab&rsquo;s table fills an unquantified remainder that NUBASE marks with a question mark. The magic numbers 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82 and 126 (filled shells in the nuclear shell model) were written from knowledge and not looked up; 126 is a neutron number only, since the chart ends at 118 protons.</li><li>The heights of the chart&rsquo;s landscape (0.11 stage 3) are a chosen scale, not a measurement and not from a source: the logarithm (base 10) of the half-life in seconds, from 10<sup>&minus;23</sup> s (flat) to 10<sup>32</sup> s (the top), which are the shortest and the longest half-lives in the lab&rsquo;s table rounded outwards to whole powers of ten; one scale height is drawn 12 tiles tall, a number chosen by looking so that the proton-rich side is not hidden behind the stable ridge. Stable nuclides have no half-life and all stand at one fixed height, 1.1 scale heights, above the top; a nuclide with no measured half-life, or only NUBASE&rsquo;s extrapolation from systematics, has no height at all (a flat hollow or pale square); a limit or an approximate value is drawn as a pale block at the limit&rsquo;s height. The projection is isometric: the chart is turned 45 degrees and tipped 54.74 degrees (arccos of 1/&radic;3) away from straight down, the standard angles that make the three axes look equally long; they were written from knowledge and not looked up.</li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">⚛️ Atoms and particles</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://www-nds.iaea.org/amdc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NUBASE and the Atomic Mass Evaluation</a> (isotopes, half-lives, binding energy)</li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1088/1674-1137/abddae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NUBASE2020 (Kondev et al., 2021)</a> (how each unstable nucleus decays and the odds of each way, for radioactive decay in the Atom Lab)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/lmmentel/mendeleev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The mendeleev Python package</a> (the data table the isotopes came through)</li><li><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Particle Data Group</a> (quark masses)</li><li>Wikipedia: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proton\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proton</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neutron\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Neutron</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_nucleus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Atomic nucleus</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bohr_model\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bohr model</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_orbital\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Atomic orbital</a></li><li><a href=\"https://zenodo.org/record/2493915\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Niels Bohr, 1913: On the constitution of atoms and molecules, Part I</a> (the 1913 paper behind the Bohr 1913 style)</li><li>Textbooks for the hydrogen-like orbitals (the average distance of the electron, the radial probability, the cloud tests): David J. Griffiths, <i>Introduction to Quantum Mechanics</i>; Hans A. Bethe and Edwin E. Salpeter, <i>Quantum Mechanics of One- and Two-Electron Atoms</i></li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">🌌 Stars and far-away things</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://github.com/astronexus/hyg-database\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HYG star database v4.1 (David Nash, astronexus)</a> (the stars and their colours, built from the Hipparcos, Yale and Gliese catalogues; licence CC BY-SA 4.0, changed here into the ecliptic coordinates of the planets)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/Stellarium/stellarium\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Stellarium: the “modern” constellation lines</a> (licence CC BY-SA 4.0)</li><li><a href=\"https://theskylive.com/voyager1-info\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Sky Live: Voyager 1</a></li><li><a href=\"https://theskylive.com/voyager2-info\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Sky Live: Voyager 2</a> (their sky positions and distances on 30 Sep 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Voyager 1</a></li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Voyager 2</a> (speeds, and the dates they reached the Sun’s wind edge)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heliosphere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the heliosphere</a> (where the Voyagers crossed the heliopause: 121 AU in 2012 and 119 AU in 2018)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha_Centauri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Alpha Centauri</a> (4.34 and 4.24 light years)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pleiades\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the Pleiades</a> (Gaia distance, 444 light years)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orion_Nebula\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the Orion Nebula</a> (1,267 light years)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sagittarius_A*\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Sagittarius A*</a> (27,000 light years)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.07478\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GRAVITY Collaboration, 2022: mass distribution in the Galactic Centre</a> (the source of the Sagittarius A* mass shown in the Orbit Lab: 4.3 million Suns)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.01636\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pourbaix &amp; Boffin, 2016: Parallax and masses of alpha Centauri revisited</a> (the masses of Alpha Centauri A and B)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.01318\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Zurlo et al., 2018: The gravitational mass of Proxima Centauri measured with SPHERE from a microlensing event</a> (the mass of Proxima Centauri)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.10625\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bond et al., 2017: The Sirius System and its Astrophysical Puzzles: Hubble Space Telescope and Ground-Based Astrometry</a> (the masses of Sirius A and B)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.00485\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bond et al., 2015: Hubble Space Telescope Astrometry of the Procyon System</a> (the masses of Procyon A and B)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6353\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Mamajek, 2012: On the Age and Binarity of Fomalhaut and its Giant Planet (ApJ 754, L20)</a> (the mass of Fomalhaut, worked out with stellar models)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.4425\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ram&iacute;rez &amp; Allende Prieto, 2011: Fundamental Parameters and Chemical Composition of Arcturus (ApJ 743, 135)</a> (the mass of Arcturus, worked out with stellar models)</li></ul><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Distances of Polaris, Betelgeuse and Rigel are uncertain, so they are rounded.</div><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The star masses are published estimates, in solar masses, and some are uncertain; the Fomalhaut and Arcturus masses come from stellar models, not from measuring an orbit. The numbers were taken from the papers&rsquo; summaries and have not been checked against their tables. Stars whose mass could not be sourced show none.</div><div class=\"srcH\">🌍 Spinning planets and dates</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10569-017-9805-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Archinal et al.: Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements, 2015</a> (the direction of each planet’s north pole and how fast it turns)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.sai.msu.ru/neb/Bidon/ChaptersPDF/nsd_App06b8_Rot.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The IAU rotational elements table (PDF)</a> (Mars to Pluto, checked against the numbers below)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/CS-SI/Orekit/blob/develop/src/main/java/org/orekit/bodies/PredefinedIAUPoles.java\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Orekit: the IAU pole and turning models as code</a> (Mercury, Venus, Earth and the small wobble terms of Mars and Neptune)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axial_tilt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: axial tilt</a> (to compare each planet’s lean)</li><li><a href=\"https://starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/StarChild/questions/question60.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA StarChild: Mars in August 2003</a> (27 Aug 2003, 55.76 million km)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_conjunction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: great conjunction</a> (Jupiter and Saturn, 21 Dec 2020, 0.1° apart)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_transit_of_Venus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the 2012 transit of Venus</a></li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halley%27s_Comet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Halley’s Comet</a> (closest on 9 Feb 1986, due back 28 Jul 2061)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discovery_of_Neptune\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the discovery of Neptune</a> (23 Sep 1846)</li></ul><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Planet places on other dates come from the NASA/JPL orbit numbers listed under Space and orbits.</div><div class=\"srcH\">☀️ The Sun, seasons and the timeline</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://github.com/CS-SI/Orekit/blob/develop/src/main/java/org/orekit/bodies/PredefinedIAUPoles.java\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Orekit: the IAU models as code</a> (the Sun’s pole, 286.13° and 63.87°, and its turning rate, 14.1844° a day)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_rotation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: solar rotation</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">the Sun</a> (the equator turns faster than the poles; the axis leans 7.25°)</li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Earth facts</a> (the 23.4° tilt, and that the tilt, not the distance, causes the seasons)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apsis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: apsis</a> (Earth’s closest and farthest distance from the Sun, with the 2026 dates)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solstice\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: solstice</a> (the 2026 equinox and solstice times)</li><li>Saturn’s ring: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christiaan_Huygens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Christiaan Huygens</a> and <a href=\"https://dwc.knaw.nl/en/?p=2435\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">the Huygens ring discovery (KNAW)</a></li><li>The Sun is not the centre of the galaxy: <a href=\"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Harlow-Shapley\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Britannica: Harlow Shapley</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galactic_Center\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Galactic Center</a></li><li>Landing on Venus and Mars: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venera_7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Venera 7</a>, <a href=\"https://solarviews.com/eng/venera7.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Solarviews: Venera 7</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viking_1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Viking 1</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Viking 1</a> (the sources disagree on how long Venera 7 transmitted, so the text gives no number)</li><li>A comet hits Jupiter: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comet_Shoemaker%E2%80%93Levy_9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9</a> and <a href=\"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Comet-Shoemaker-Levy-9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Britannica</a></li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Voyager 1</a> (leaving the Sun’s wind, 25 Aug 2012, 121 AU)</li><li>Comet landing and Ceres: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosetta_(spacecraft)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Rosetta</a>, <a href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/rosettas-philae-makes-historic-first-landing-on-a-comet/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: Philae’s landing</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dawn_(spacecraft)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Dawn</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Dawn</a></li><li>Touching the Sun: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parker_Solar_Probe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Parker Solar Probe</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/parker-solar-probe/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Parker Solar Probe</a> (the corona crossing was on 28 Apr 2021 and announced in Dec 2021)</li><li>Weighing the black hole: <a href=\"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2020/press-release/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S2_(star)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the star S2</a></li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">🧪 Everyday element facts</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_chemical_elements\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the element pages</a> (uses, melting points, abundance)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abundance_of_the_chemical_elements\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: abundance of the elements</a> (what the universe and Earth’s crust are made of)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/107536-highest-electrical-conductivity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Guinness World Records: highest electrical conductivity</a> (silver)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iridium_anomaly\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the iridium layer</a> (the asteroid that ended the dinosaurs)</li></ul></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Version: Physics Lab 8.6.2. Labs: Orbit Lab 7.7.2 · Atom Lab 7.7.3.</p>";
static const char* const kHistoryTitle = "Physics Lab · history";
static const char* const kHistoryIntro = "How Physics Lab itself has grown, newest first. Each lab also keeps its own detailed changelog: click the Update badge at the top right.";
static const char* const kHistoryNote = "The earliest entries were put together from memory, so the order and the numbers may be slightly off.";
static const char* const kHistoryHere = " · you are here";
static const char* const kHistoryYear = "Physics Lab ";   // + version
struct LogEntry { const char* v; const char* ic; const char* t; const char* d; const char* labButton; const char* lab; };
static const LogEntry kAppLog[] = {
  {"8.6.2", "🛠", "Unlock everything (for testing)", "Settings has a new Developer section with one button, Unlock everything. It unlocks every secret theme and every achievement at once, with no pop-ups, so they are easy to test. The Reset my progress button above it locks them all again.", "", ""},
  {"8.6.1", "🧪", "Bigger table without scrolling", "The ⤢ Bigger table pop-up is now wide enough to show the whole periodic table at once, with no sideways scroll bar. On a phone the buttons shrink to fit.", "", ""},
  {"8.6", "🧹", "Tidy-up: readable, bigger, closer", "Four small fixes from a design review. The facts written on top of the picture now sit on a soft see-through panel, so they stay readable even over bright clouds and stars. The periodic table has a new ⤢ Bigger table button that opens it large, with buttons big enough for a finger, scrolled to the element you have loaded. The small grey text all over the app is one step bigger (Settings still scales it). And in the Atom Lab the zoom buttons (Atom, Nucleus, Nucleon, Centre), Drag and Reset view now sit right under the picture instead of in a card further down.", "", ""},
  {"8.5.1", "✨", "Reduced motion", "Settings has a new Motion choice: Auto, Reduced or Full. Reduced is for anyone who feels dizzy or distracted when a lot moves on screen. Labs start paused with a ▶ Play button, camera moves jump straight to the end instead of gliding, Cloud 3D does not spin by itself, the Did you know facts change only when you tap them, and pop-ups appear without sliding or fading. The physics stays exactly the same. Auto (the default) follows the device’s own “reduce motion” setting. Your choice is remembered between visits.", "", ""},
  {"8.5", "✨", "Unit switch", "Settings has a new Units choice: Metric or Imperial. It changes the distances, speeds and temperatures the labs work out: planet, moon, comet and star cards in the Orbit Lab, its scale bar, the Black Hole readout, element melting and boiling points, the room temperature line and atom speeds in the Atom Lab, and the size comparisons in the Atom Lab Scale guide. Science units (nm, K, kg) and the written guides stay as they are. Your choice is remembered between visits.", "", ""},
  {"8.4.6", "✨", "Spectrum, decay history and lighter orbits", "Atom Lab 7.5 brings a Light spectrum card, a Decay history card and half-lives in the periodic table. Orbit Lab 7.7.1 draws its trails with less work and looks exactly the same.", "", ""},
  {"8.4.5", "✨", "Half-Life achievement", "A new achievement: watch an unstable nucleus decay in the Atom Lab. Radioactive decay arrived in Atom Lab 7: every unstable nucleus now decays on its own at its real half-life.", "", ""},
  {"8.4.4", "✨", "Smoother frames in every lab", "Every frame, the app used to rewrite the hint text and the buttons and re-read the theme colours, even when nothing had changed, which made the browser lay out the whole page again 60 times a second. Now it only touches the page when something really changes, and reads the colours only when you change the theme. Also in this update: Orbit Lab 7.7 (faster at high time speed) and Black Hole Lab 3.3 (spin labels fixed, lighter when paused) and Spacetime Lab 2.6.1 (a crash with heavy neutron stars fixed).", "", ""},
  {"8.4.3", "✨", "Help guides on one page", "The Orbit, Atom and Spacetime Lab help guides are no longer dropdown boxes. Each is one long page with its headings kept, a row of tappable headings at the top to jump around, and a small ↑ on every heading to come back.", "", ""},
  {"8.4.2", "✨", "Learn tab keeps you on the Learn tab", "Tapping a guide or timeline in the 📚 Learn tab now opens it right there instead of switching you into that lab. Close it and you are back on the Learn list, in the lab you were in before.", "", ""},
  {"8.4.1", "✨", "Sources for the Sun, seasons and the new timeline steps", "The About page now lists where the Orbit Lab’s newest facts come from: the Sun’s real spin, why Earth has seasons (with the 2026 equinox and solstice times), and each of the new Solar System timeline steps, from Huygens and Saturn’s ring to the 2020 Nobel Prize for weighing the black hole at the centre of our galaxy.", "", ""},
  {"8.4", "✨", "Galactic Center achievement", "A fourth achievement: click the black hole at the centre of our galaxy (Sagittarius A*) in the Orbit Lab. Zoom a very long way out, or tap it under Far away, and it is yours.", "", ""},
  {"8.3", "🌍", "Sources for the Orbit Lab’s spinning planets", "The About page now lists where the Orbit Lab’s spinning planets and its Jump to a date come from: the IAU report on how each planet turns and leans, and the pages behind the famous dates (Mars in 2003, the 2020 great conjunction, the 2012 transit of Venus, Halley’s Comet and the discovery of Neptune).", "", ""},
  {"8.2", "📚", "Sources for the Orbit Lab’s stars", "The About page now credits the star data behind the Orbit Lab’s new night sky and far-away things (the HYG star database and Stellarium’s constellation lines, both CC BY-SA 4.0) and lists the sources for the Voyagers and the far-away objects.", "", ""},
  {"8.1", "📚", "Sources for the Spacetime Lab", "The About page now lists the scientific sources behind the Spacetime Lab: the papers and pages for the Schwarzschild spacetime, the shape of space, the marble paths and clock slowing, and the measurements of the real objects (the Sun, the Moon, the heavy neutron star, Cygnus X-1, Sagittarius A* and M87*).", "", ""},
  {"8", "🌀", "Spacetime Lab", "A fourth simulation: gravity as bent spacetime. A grid shows the bend around a star, a black hole, a planet, a Moon, a human or a basketball, and you can roll marbles across it to see them circle, fall in or fly by. A Bend setting switches between Easy to see and True proportions (a human makes no dent at all), and the readout shows each object’s real mass, size, horizon radius and escape speed. A guide explains what everything is and what the picture gets wrong, and it is in the Learn tab too (Spacetime Lab 1).", "▶ Open Spacetime Lab", "Spacetime Lab"},
  {"7.6", "🔘", "Buttons instead of drop-down menus", "Every setting that used to open a drop-down menu is now a row of buttons you can always see, with the one in use highlighted: the electron pictures in the Atom Lab (Orbit, Cloud, Cloud 3D, Slice), Orbit style, quark style, On and Off switches in every lab, Quality in the Black Hole Lab, and more. A small note under the row explains the chosen option. The lab picker at the top and the Famous atoms list in the Atom Lab are still menus, because their lists are longer.", "", ""},
  {"7.5", "📚", "Learn tab, Learn cards higher up", "A new 📚 Learn tab sits next to Simulations, Games and Achievements. It lists every guide and timeline (the Atom Lab’s guide, Scale, Weight and Timeline, and the Orbit Lab’s guide and Timeline). Tap one and it opens in its lab. The Learn card in the Orbit Lab and the Atom Lab also moved up: it is now the third card in the Orbit Lab (under Time speed) and the second card in the Atom Lab (under the periodic table). On phones the four tabs now sit in two rows so none is cut off.", "", ""},
  {"7.1", "🔒", "Clearer progress reset", "Settings → Progress → Reset my progress now opens a pop-up page. It says this is permanent and lists exactly what will be locked again (for example 2 secret themes and 3 achievements). After you confirm, a second pop-up says Progress reset with what was locked, and the Settings page shows a highlighted Done line. If you have nothing unlocked yet, it tells you there is nothing to reset.", "", ""},
  {"7", "🏆", "Achievements", "A new 🏆 Achievements tab next to Simulations and Games shows every achievement, locked until you earn it. When you earn one, a small pop-up slides in at the corner of the screen for a few seconds and goes away by itself, so it never blocks what you are doing. The first three: Neon Nights (find the secret Synthwave theme), Sharp Eyes (find the secret Monochrome theme) and Point of No Return (zoom all the way in on a black hole). Settings → Progress now locks the achievements again too. Also new: the About page lists sources for the Black Hole Lab, the moons and Bohr’s 1913 paper.", "", ""},
  {"6.9", "⚛️", "Atom Lab: Bohr 1913 style, plus many fixes", "The Orbit view has a new Orbit style setting. “Bohr 1913” draws the atom the way Niels Bohr drew it: one small nucleus ball with a plus sign, the fixed circles n = 1 to 4, and one electron ball. No neutrons, no quarks. ↓ Emit photon and ↑ Absorb photon make the electron leap between circles with a flash. The Modern style is still there. Also fixed the photon label running off the edge of the picture on phones. Also in this update: the Black Hole Lab got a Quality setting (Auto, High, Medium and Low, so phones run smoother) and rendering fixes (a dotted line through the picture, a hairline seam, a broken patch when looking down on the disc; the Viewing angle now starts at 20°). The Orbit Lab lets go of a planet without a jump, zooms deep more smoothly (the Ceres lag is fixed), starts every belt view the same way, and has short tails with a new Trails setting. Slider knobs now always drag, and the update list no longer shows “undefined” on phones.", "", ""},
  {"6.8", "❓", "FPS counter, exact comet orbits, progress reset and Orbit guide", "Every lab now shows its frames per second in the top right corner of the picture. In Orbit Lab the comets stay exactly on their orbit line at any zoom (before, they slid off it and seemed to bounce when zoomed far in). Settings has a new Progress part with a Reset button that locks the secret themes again, after an “are you sure” step. I also timed both Orbit Lab and Atom Lab across all their views, zoom levels and time speeds and tried random taps and drags: no other slow parts or errors turned up. Orbit Lab now has the same ❓ guide as Atom Lab. It explains in plain words what every ring, dot, line and colour on the picture means, what is to scale and what is not, what Time speed means, why the Sun wobbles, and how comets get their tails. It also shows live facts about what you have open and selected.", "", ""},
  {"6.7", "☄️", "Orbit Lab: scale bar, famous comets, no lag near the Sun, and a secret theme", "A scale bar with light travel time, and six famous comets on their real orbits with glowing heads and tails: Halley, Hale–Bopp, NEOWISE, Encke, 67P and Swift–Tuttle. A new secret colour theme hides somewhere in the app. It stays a ??? in the 🎨 Theme list until you find it. Clue: read the Atom Lab guide closely. Zooming in close to the Sun (or anywhere the comets' dotted orbits run far off screen) made the app crawl. Only the part of each comet orbit that is on screen is drawn now, so it stays smooth.", "", ""},
  {"6.6", "🪐", "Orbit Lab: real-looking planets, Sun and true scale", "Planets, moons and the Sun became lit balls, then smooth single-colour balls with a soft glowing Sun that spins. The Orbit Lab now starts at 1 s = 29 days, and moons follow the Time speed properly. A new Barycentre marker shows the real balance point of the Sun and planets, which the Sun wobbles around. True scale: planets, moons and the Sun are drawn at their real size, zoom goes up to ×300,000, Earth has real continents and clouds, and there is no minimum dot any more (bodies smaller than a pixel are marked with a small ring). A Sizes setting brings back the bigger planets. The Time speed slider moved up, right under Reset to today.", "", ""},
  {"6.5", "🚀", "Orbit Lab: Go to table", "A new Go to card in Orbit Lab with a tile for the Sun, every planet, dwarf planet and moon, and the belts and the far edge. Tap one and the view flies there. Trails behind far-away bodies, the dwarf planets most of all, no longer lag behind and jump: they always reach the body. The note at the top left of the picture now shows the real date as time passes, for example 29 Sep 2126 after 100 years. Going to a planet, moon or belt no longer changes the time speed you chose. The mouse wheel zooms in smaller steps in Orbit Lab, so far-away dwarf planets are easier to reach. The ring around the selected planet, moon or Sun is now bright pink with a dark edge. Belts and the Oort cloud light up in pink with a name tag when you go to them. Tap a selected Go to tile again to deselect it. Orbit Lab now has the little x y z axes at the bottom right, like Atom Lab.", "", ""},
  {"6.4", "🧪", "Atom Lab: steadier quarks, sharper info button, element card", "Fixed the quark triplets that flickered, disappeared and reappeared when you looked closely at a nucleus. Quark triplets no longer appear and disappear out of thin air in big nuclei: they fade in and out smoothly as you turn or move the picture. The ⓘ info button in Atom Lab is now a sharp, drawn icon, a little bigger and easier to tap. Every element now has a card under the periodic table: its tile, family, period and group, state at room temperature, and a plain-words line about where you meet it in daily life.", "", ""},
  {"6.3", "⚙️", "Language, About, Settings and sources", "A new 🌐 button in the header lets you pick a language: English works now, and Estonian is marked “Coming soon” (the translation is next on the planned list). A new 👤 About button tells you who makes Physics Lab, what it is built with and where the facts come from. A new ⚙ Settings button in the header. It has a Text size setting (small, normal, large, extra large) and a Font setting (theme default, serif, easy to read, rounded, monospace). Both change the text across the whole app, including the labels drawn inside the labs, and are remembered. More settings can go there later. The About page now links to the main sources behind Physics Lab’s facts: NASA/JPL for the planets, NUBASE and the Particle Data Group for atoms and particles, and Wikipedia and other pages for the everyday element facts.", "", ""},
  {"6.2", "❓", "Atom Lab: “What am I even looking at?” and every view draggable", "A new ❓ button in Atom Lab explains the atom on screen in plain words, with sections you can open and close: nucleus, protons, neutrons, quarks, the electron, and what each picture and number means. It follows the element you pick. All four electron views can be dragged now. Orbit turns in 3D, Cloud slides around, Slice moves its cut through the cloud, and Cloud 3D rotates as before. Fixed a bug where the electron cloud seemed to disappear: moving the picture and then zooming in could push it off screen. It now always stays in view.", "", ""},
  {"6.1", "🌙", "Orbit Lab: moons stay visible", "Fixed a bug where moons (Earth’s Moon, the moons of Mars and others) disappeared at some angles in the 3D view.", "", ""},
  {"6", "🎨", "Colour themes and the “Did you know?” line", "A line at the top of the screen shows a physics fact and swaps to a new one every few seconds. Tap it to skip to the next fact. There are 25 facts about space, temperature and atoms. A new 🎨 Theme button in the header opens a short list: Auto (follows your device), Light, Dark, Black, Midnight, Solar and Paper. Your choice is remembered on this device. All labs use the new colours. Sage (a calm, cool grey-green) and High contrast (black with white text and yellow accents) join the 🎨 Theme list. The list also scrolls now when the screen is short, such as a phone held sideways. The fact line now shows which fact you are on (like 3/26) and waits while you hold it with your finger or hover over it with the mouse, so you can finish reading. There is one new fact too, and it is a surprise. There is a new locked slot at the bottom of the 🎨 Theme list, and now 27 facts in the 💡 line. Keep reading the facts to find out how to open it.", "", ""},
  {"5.6", "⚖", "Weight panel", "A new Learn panel in Atom Lab about weight: what an atom and its parts weigh, the missing mass and binding energy, how many atoms are in everyday things, why a proton’s weight is mostly not quarks, and a mass ladder from the electron to the Sun (Atom Lab 5.6).", "▶ Open Atom Lab", "Atom Lab"},
  {"5.5", "🌡", "Temperature in the Atom Lab", "Every element shows its melting and boiling point, state at room temperature and density. A temperature slider shows atoms as a solid, liquid or gas (Atom Lab 5.5).", "▶ Open Atom Lab", "Atom Lab"},
  {"5.1", "🕰", "Physics Lab history", "A timeline of the app itself, opened from the 🕰 button next to the title. It lists every big step so far, with a ▶ button that jumps to the lab or game it is about, and what is planned next.", "", ""},
  {"5", "☉", "Orbit Lab: the real Solar System", "Orbit Lab was simplified to just the real Solar System, placed where everything is today. It got a 21-step timeline from wandering stars to DART, and time speed that goes down to real time (Orbit Lab 2.5).", "▶ Open Orbit Lab", "Orbit Lab"},
  {"4.6", "⚛️", "A realistic nucleus, and phone fixes", "Protons and neutrons are placed like a real nucleus (random, non-overlapping, neutron skin, measured deformation such as uranium’s rugby-ball shape). Shorter readout with an ⓘ button, and long text wraps on phones in every lab (Atom Lab 5).", "▶ Open Atom Lab", "Atom Lab"},
  {"4.5", "🎓", "Learn panels", "Atom Lab gained a scale comparison (“if the nucleus were a football …”), a powers-of-ten ruler and a 22-step timeline of how we pictured the atom, with buttons that jump to the matching atom (Atom Lab 4.5).", "▶ Open Atom Lab", "Atom Lab"},
  {"4", "🚀", "The first game: Slingshot", "A game inside the lab: launch a spacecraft from Earth on a real date, aim by dragging, and reach a planet with limited fuel. Six missions from Venus to Neptune’s orbit, a quiz for each, stars for fuel saved, and a Simulations / Games switch at the top (Slingshot 1).", "▶ Play Slingshot", "Slingshot"},
  {"3.5", "📷", "Save image", "A 📷 Save image button in every lab saves exactly what you see as a PNG (Atom Lab 4.1).", "", ""},
  {"3", "🕳️", "Black Hole Lab", "The third lab: a black hole with a glowing accretion disc that bends light, drawn on the GPU (with a slower fallback), and then spin, using the equations for a rotating Kerr black hole (Black Hole Lab 1–3).", "▶ Open Black Hole Lab", "Black Hole Lab"},
  {"2.5", "🧪", "The periodic table and isotopes", "Atom Lab: click any of the 118 elements, with real half-lives for all 3,557 known isotopes, X-rays for heavy atoms and realistic quarks with colour charge (Atom Lab 3.5–4).", "", ""},
  {"2", "⚛️", "Atom Lab", "A second lab appeared, so a lab menu was added. It began with a nucleus and one electron on an orbit, then grew a probability cloud, a rotatable 3D view, a zoom down to the nucleus and a zoom into the quarks (Atom Lab 1–3). Each lab now shows its own Update number, and clicking it opens that lab’s changelog. Your settings are remembered between visits, with a Defaults button.", "▶ Open Atom Lab", "Atom Lab"},
  {"1", "✨", "Physics Lab is born", "Orbit Lab: a star with planets you can add and launch, then a 3D view and click-for-info on anything. Then the real planets from NASA/JPL data with sizes, masses and gravity, real moons, the asteroid belt, Kuiper belt, dwarf planets and the Oort cloud (Orbit Lab 1–2).", "", ""}};
